WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.947
For this guided practice, I want you to predict the result before you run the query.

00:00:04.947 --> 00:00:09.157
We compare two versions of a mixed AND and OR rule.

00:00:09.157 --> 00:00:14.472
In the unparenthesized version, AND binds more tightly than OR.

00:00:14.472 --> 00:00:19.297
That means the consent requirement applies only to one branch of the logic.

00:00:19.297 --> 00:00:25.332
In the parenthesized version, the age condition and the diagnosis condition are grouped together first,

00:00:25.332 --> 00:00:28.647
and then consent is required for the entire group.

00:00:28.647 --> 00:00:34.297
Look for the patient who satisfies the age condition but has false consent.

00:00:34.297 --> 00:00:37.827
That patient is the key to understanding the difference.

00:00:37.827 --> 00:00:43.462
Do not stop at saying, “Version A returns one extra row.” Explain why.

00:00:43.462 --> 00:00:50.057
A good explanation is: the age branch is evaluated independently of the consent test in Version A because

00:00:50.057 --> 00:00:53.512
the AND belongs to the diagnosis branch.

00:00:53.512 --> 00:00:59.837
In Version B, parentheses make consent apply to both age and diagnosis alternatives.

00:00:59.837 --> 00:01:03.502
This is exactly how I want you to reason in real work.

00:01:03.502 --> 00:01:08.652
When two queries produce different populations, identify the record that changes and trace that record through

00:01:08.652 --> 00:01:15.523
the Boolean expression. That is much more informative than staring at the whole result set.
